CLUE Newsletter, September 1995 

The Colorado Linux Users & Enthusiasts ( CLUE ), normally meets the third 
Thursday of every month. The next meeting will be at 7:00pm on September 21
(a third Thursday). The location will be Smoky Hill High School, located at 
16100 E. Smoky Hill Rd. in southeast Aurora. CLUE is for anyone with an 
interest in Linux, whether you are an Expert, or just interested and want 
to know more about the Internet and Operating System phenomena that Linux 
represents. All interested parties are invited to attend. Let's learn from 
each other!!

For more information, or to be added/removed from the CLUE mailing list
contact James at 303-699-7470 or E-Mail: clue@wgs.com, or attend a meeting.

------

Future Meeting dates will be:
Q3/95  September 21, 1995, 
Q4/95  October 19, 1995,  November 9, 1995,  December 21, 1995
Q1/96  January 18, 1996,  February 15, 1996,  March 21, 1996
Q2/96  April 18, 1996,  May 16, 1996,  June 20, 1996

Directions to Smoky Hill High School:  Take Parker south from I-225, to 
Quincy.  There will be a shopping center on the left, and a Stoplight. Take 
a left on Quincy. Then the first stoplight with a right turn will be Smoky 
Hill Road.  Turn right onto Smoky Hill, cross Chambers (a light), and then 
turn right at Laredo (a stop light), then left into the School parking lot.  
Go past the main entrance, and to the activities entrance.

Door prizes will be given away at every meeting.

------

HOT NEWS FLASH  and/or Current News

At the August meeting, Steve Martin was appointed President Pro-Tem of 
CLUE. What this means, is that he will act as president until the 
membership chooses to bother with elections, or until he quits or gets 
lynched, whichever comes first.  Other officer volunteers / appointees
will be elected / appointed as they can be found or are needed.
Steve can be contacted at smartin@mrg.uswest.com

Joel Noble volunteered that it would be a good idea to run a CLUE club 
library, and then subsequently became the appointed CLUE librarian.
Joel can be contacted at joel_noble@msmgate.mrg.uswest.com

Early next year Rich Maginn and his students will be presenting an 
Internet / Worldwide Web hands on workshop using the Smoky Hill network.
This will be available to anyone who cares to attend that particular CLUE 
meeting. There may be a donation requested to help Smoky Hill buy more
computer equipment, this has not yet been determined. More information to 
come, stay tuned!

------

Next Meeting Topic (Sep 21, 1995):

At the September meeting Steve Martin will give an overview of some of the
more popular distributions of Linux, what they are, how they differ, and what
features they offer. He will discuss how the installation process works for
each, and what major software packages are included. In addition he will take
a look at some of the CD producers and talk about which distributions they
offer, and what additional items they might provide beyond the standard
distributions. Definitely included will be Slackware and Red Hat, and possibly
other distributions too. 

If there is time, Steve will also give a quick overview of X11 for Linux, and
explore the mystery of how to configure it properly after it has been
installed.

We look forward to your comments and questions!

------

Meeting Topics, Newsletter Articles:
We would like your ideas for presenters/speakers, and topics for both 
Meeting presentations, and also Newsletter articles.  Please help; supply 
either or both for us.  Volunteers are especially appreciated.

Notes from the last meeting held on Aug 17, 1995

The Smoky Hill Internet Project
by Rich Maginn

Smoky Hill High School's Internet project was the subject of the last
Colorado Linux Users Enthusiast(CLUE) meeting.  Smoky Hill teacher/Computer
Coordinator, Rich Maginn, gave a history of the project and described to the
audience the role Linux has played as the foundation for an efficent cost
effective network server solution in supporting their Internet project.

Smoky Hill currently runs 3-486 Linux boxes providing everything from Domain
Name Service, UUCP, Internet News, mail, WWW server and a wide variety of
other applications necessary in supporting the curriculum needs of the
faculty and students.  Complete technical descriptions are available at
http://www.smoky.ccsd.k12.co.us/more_html/sh_servers.html.  

Rich Maginn used the time to describe the service students provide in
managing the network.  A student intern program gives them training in
building, configuring and maintaining our Linux servers from the ground up. 
The servers provide services for the 150+ Mac Ethernet network at Smoky Hill.
The school is currently supporting close to 1000 email accounts.  Over the
last year, there was over 375,000 hours of use on the UUCP based mail system.
Accesses off of our WWW page during a 24 hour period peek around 2000.  Our
WWW server is currently being moved to one of the Linux machines using Apache
httpd software.  We currently run the Cern www server software.

Here are some interesting highlights and student accomplishments of 
Smoky Hill Students using Internet resources: six student interns script 
USWest projects for the Internet; four Smoky Hill students were the opening 
presentors at the Second Annual Computer Ethics Conference at the 
Brookings Institute in Washington;  twelve students will begin work 
on a state project called "Under the Cyberdome" to try to extend youth
participation in government; Smoky WWW, our World Wide Web page is 
recognized by the Dutch Ministry of Education January School of the 
Month - the first school to be recognized for the award; and the 
Communications Association of America recognizes our Information
Technology page in their K-12 resources.  Check out Awards and 
Recognition on our Web site for more.  The audience took a tour of 
the facility.  Questions and answers followed.  It was agreed that 
the Smoky Hill facility would be ideal for subsequent CLUE meetings 
regarding the use of Linux and the Internet.  

Project History:  Just over two and a half years ago Smoky Hill High 
School in Aurora, Colorado embarked on this project to demonstrate 
the use of Information Technology as a tool in research and curriculum 
of our academic programs.  Throught the efforts and energy from 
faculty, administrative support, and community interest the Smoky Hill 
Internet Project is now a reality.  Many inter-discplinary curriculum 
projects have been created at our Web site:
  http://www.smoky.ccsd.k12.co.us/  
visit to get a glimpse of the school.  This page was included as one of 
the K-12 site in the latest release of the "Internet Unleashed".  

Please visit and send us a note.

----
Editor's note: The above was written by Rich Maginn, and edited a bit by
me.  I believe that Rich has been too modest, so I am going to add a few 
things I happen to know about.

The Cherry Creek School District Information Systems people are jealous
of Rich, and say that many of his students are worth about $45K each in 
setting and administering the Smoky Hill information system.  Rich and his 
students have actually accomplished what the Information systems people have 
been trying to do for years, but made little progress on due to lack of 
educated manpower!

Rich's students in beginning classes learn word processing & graphics, and
by the end of the first class each student creates their own web page, and
then learns to access everyone else' web pages, and watch the hits happen.
Among other CIS classes at Smoky Hill there is also an advanced placement 
computer course which starts with beginning programming, and goes through
structured programming, data structures, binary trees, sorts and much more.
This class runs 2 semesters, and CU will give a full semester of CIS
credit to students who pass it.  MIT however grants a FULL YEAR.  How's
that for a prophet not being accepted in his own country?

One young man took this class, and then did some extra work in object 
oriented programming with C++.  He then went to attend the Colorado
School of Mines.  The school gave him 3 full years of CIS credit, and
granted him senior status on his first year there.  Another young lady
was offerred a 50,000 dollar per year job directly out of High School,
due to what she learned at Smoky Hill.  There are more examples like 
this.  

Smoky Hill's system consists of about 180 Macs, and 3 Linux Systems, with
the Linux systems providing internet and mail services to the entire net.
The system was down only three times all of last school year, once for
2 minutes, once for 12 minutes, and once for 12 hours.  This is an 
incredible uptime record. If businesses with professional administrators 
would only do so well!

At a recent conference, John Patrick, IBM's VP of Internet apps said that 
he has a list of 8 favorite internet sites, and Smoky Hill is on that list!

Rich says that if they could only get about 30 or so more 386 and 486
class computers they could nearly double their CIS program enrollment.
So if you have hardware that is being chucked with an upgrade, think of
Smoky Hill for a tax deductible contribution.

As for the technology portions of the curriculum, they are not Macintosh
based, they are real world, and technology based.  In the real world
MacIntoshes must live as the minority in a PC majority world, and all
computer systems can mix & match if it is done well.  Smoky Hill High 
school has.

Rich Maginn is one teacher really making a difference.  Too bad the rest 
of our education system in the US does not do as well. He has one statement
he makes often.  (Editor taking liberties with it)  "Parents, forget the 
brand name on the computer, and encourage your kids to learn about 
technology and what it can be used for so that they will be ready in 
the real world they will have to deal with.  It will be a much more 
complex and technological world than what you as parents have come 
to know."
 
So which High School would you prefer your child attend?  Smoky Hill is
definately a good choice.  But wouldn't it be better if all the schools
could follow Smoky Hill's example?  That is where YOU can make a 
difference.  Get involved!!!

