Announcing xearth version 1.0. From the README file:

  Xearth sets the X root window to an image of the Earth, as seen from
  your favorite vantage point in space, correctly shaded for the
  current position of the Sun. By default, xearth updates the
  displayed image every five minutes; the time between updates can be
  changed using either X resources or a command line option. Xearth
  can also render directly into PPM and GIF files instead of drawing
  in the root window; see the man page for details.

What's new since version 0.92? Lots of stuff, including:

  - new position specifier (random)
  - mercator projections (-proj)
  - rotated views (-rot)
  - shading discontinuity at terminator (-term)
  - rendering X image only once (-once)
  - user-specifiable marker information (-markerfile)
  - user-specifiable marker alignment (align=xxx)
  - user-specifiable label position (-labelpos)
  - mix of single- and double-pixel stars (-bigstars)
  - support for new display depths (12, 15, 16, and 24 bits)

See the man page and HISTORY file for details.

Sources for xearth version 1.0 (tar-ed and gzip-ed, 150 kbytes) can be
obtained via a link from the xearth home page on the World Wide Web
(URL http://cag-www.lcs.mit.edu/~tuna/xearth/index.html). Alternately,
it can be obtained via anonymous ftp from:

  cag.lcs.mit.edu:/pub/tuna/xearth-1.0.tar.gz

Share and enjoy,

kirk
